            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The scoop-size row explains most of the others. FlavCity's 49.5 g scoop is about 40 percent heavier than Just Ingredients' 35.5 g, and the extra weight is mostly food: cocoa, coconut milk powder, banana powder, sea salt, cordyceps and reishi. That is why FlavCity has 13 g of carbohydrate and 3 g of fiber against 5 g and under 1 g. Just Ingredients, by contrast, gets its calories from fat: 7 g per scoop, mainly from organic coconut milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution on the Just Ingredients column. Our <a href='/just-ingredients-protein-powder-review/'>Just Ingredients review</a> read a vanilla panel on Whole Foods and Amazon listings showing about 22 g of protein in a 33 g scoop with roughly 140 calories, sweetened with stevia and monk fruit. The French Vanilla panel on the brand site shows 35.5 g, 160 calories and monk fruit only. Flavors and listings differ, so read the panel on the bag you buy. Neither brand publishes a leucine figure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="collagen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much of Each Scoop Is Collagen?
          </h2>

          <p>
            FlavCity says about 10 g of each scoop is grass-fed collagen; Just Ingredients lists collagen in its blend but does not say how much. That is the single biggest difference in how far you can trust each protein number, and it is the question neither brand's front label answers clearly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen matters because it is an incomplete protein. It has no tryptophan, an essential amino acid, and it is low in leucine, the amino acid most tied to triggering muscle protein synthesis. Grams of collagen count toward the protein total on a nutrition panel, but they do less for muscle repair than the same grams of whey. Collagen is not useless; it is simply a different tool, marketed mostly for skin and joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            For FlavCity the arithmetic is possible. Its panel lists 25 g of total protein and the brand states 10 g of collagen per scoop, so the collagen appears to be counted inside the 25 g. On that reading, a scoop carries about 15 g of complete protein from whey concentrate, milk protein concentrate and yeast protein. FlavCity's Amazon store, run through the seller Pattern, titled its listings 20 g of protein plus 10 g of collagen when our database was checked on 19 November 2026, which does not match the 25 g panel. We use the panel throughout.
          </p>

          <p>
            For Just Ingredients the arithmetic is not possible. Ingredients are listed by weight, and collagen sits third on the French Vanilla list, after whey concentrate and coconut milk but ahead of pea and chia protein. That tells you whey is the largest protein source and collagen is probably a minority share, but not the gram figure. Pea and chia protein are plant proteins that also count toward the 22 g. So Just Ingredients likely has more whey per scoop than FlavCity's roughly 15 g of complete protein, but we cannot prove it from the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            If collagen is the reason you are buying, FlavCity is the one that tells you what you get. If collagen is an extra you would rather not pay for, neither brand is ideal, and a single-ingredient whey from our <a href='/best-clean-protein-powder/'>best clean protein powder</a> list avoids the question entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Has More Protein per Scoop?
          </h2>

          <p>
            FlavCity has 3 g more protein per scoop, 25 g against 22 g, but Just Ingredients packs more protein into each gram of powder, about 62 percent against 51 percent. The per-scoop figure is printed biggest on the bag, yet it mostly reflects scoop size. FlavCity's scoop is 14 g heavier, so it would be surprising if it did not carry more total protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scale both to the same dose and the picture changes. To reach 25 g of protein from Just Ingredients you need about 1.14 scoops, roughly 40 g of powder and 182 calories. From FlavCity one scoop gets you there in 49.5 g and 200 calories. So Just Ingredients is slightly leaner per gram of protein, by about 18 calories, even though its scoop carries more fat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Complete protein tilts it further. If FlavCity's 10 g of collagen is inside the 25 g, its complete protein is about 15 g per scoop. Just Ingredients does not disclose its collagen share, but with whey listed first and collagen behind coconut milk, it is reasonable to expect more than 15 g of whey, pea and chia protein in a 22 g scoop. That is an inference from ingredient order, not a measured figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            For general daily protein intake, total grams are what count, and FlavCity's 25 g is a full serving. For recovery after training, research on whey generally uses 20 to 25 g of high-quality protein, so neither scoop on its own is ideal. Mixing either one with dairy milk instead of water adds about 8 g of complete protein per cup and closes most of that gap.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Is Cheaper per Gram of Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Just Ingredients is cheaper per gram of protein: about $2.46 per 25 g against $3.00 for FlavCity at brand-site prices. A $64.99 Just Ingredients bag holds 30 scoops of 22 g, or 660 g of protein. A $59.99 FlavCity bag holds 20 scoops of 25 g, or 500 g. FlavCity's bag costs $5 less but holds 160 g less protein, so you pay more for each gram.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Cost Math</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$2.46</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Just Ingredients per 25 g (30 servings)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$3.00</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">FlavCity per 25 g (20 servings)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~$16</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Monthly gap at one 25 g serving a day</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2.55</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">FlavCity per 25 g at its Amazon store price</p>
