            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            At one 25 g serving a day for 30 days, that is about $90 for FlavCity against about $74 for Just Ingredients, a gap of roughly $16 a month or close to $200 a year. Per scoop the gap is wider than it looks on the shelf: about $3.00 a FlavCity scoop against about $2.17 for Just Ingredients, because the cheaper-looking FlavCity bag holds ten fewer servings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Retailer changes the ranking a little. FlavCity's official Amazon store listed the 20-serving bag at $50.99 on 19 November 2026, which comes to about $2.55 per 25 g on the 25 g panel, still above Just Ingredients' brand-site price. Just Ingredients is also sold on Amazon in 20-serving tubs, per our review, and at Whole Foods, Sprouts and Target, but we did not record a current price for those sizes, so we cannot say whether they beat the 30-serving bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only count complete protein, the gap grows. Exclude FlavCity's 10 g of collagen and each $3.00 scoop buys about 15 g of whey, milk and yeast protein, which is about $5.00 per 25 g of complete protein. We cannot run the same sum for Just Ingredients because its collagen figure is not published. To check any listing yourself, divide the price by servings, divide by grams of protein per serving, and multiply by 25.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is FlavCity or Just Ingredients Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Just Ingredients is the one with a certification you can check: the <a href='https://www.nsfsport.com/certified-products/search-results.php' rel='nofollow noopener' target='_blank'>NSF Certified for Sport registry</a> listed five Just Ingredients protein flavors when our database was checked on 1 November 2026: Chocolate, Creamy Peanut Butter Chocolate, Snickerdoodle, Strawberries and Cream, and Vanilla Bean. FlavCity's product pages make no testing claim, and our <a href='/flavcity-protein-powder-review/'>FlavCity review</a> found no NSF, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Just Ingredients listing is flavor-specific. French Vanilla, the flavor in our spec table, was not on the registry even though its bag image shows an NSF logo, and the listed Vanilla Bean was out of stock on the brand site when we checked. Our older Just Ingredients review said we could not confirm NSF status; the registry check in our database is newer, and it is what this page relies on. If you are drug tested, search the registry for your exact flavor before ordering.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF Certified for Sport tests finished products for substances banned in sport and checks that the contents match the label, per NSF's description of the program. That makes it a stronger signal than a brand saying it tests in-house, because the buyer can look the result up. It does not cover flavors that are not named, and it says nothing about taste or value.
          </p>

          <p>
            FlavCity's bag carries a California Proposition 65 warning for lead and cadmium. That is a legal notice, not a lab result: California's thresholds are very low, and cocoa and other plant ingredients commonly trigger it. Our database has no published heavy-metal figures for either product, so we cannot say which is lower in metals. That is a gap in the data, not a finding against either brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Has the Cleaner Label?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Just Ingredients has the shorter, more protein-focused label, while FlavCity has the more food-like one. Per the French Vanilla panel, Just Ingredients lists nine ingredients: grass-fed whey concentrate, organic coconut milk, grass-fed collagen, organic pea protein, organic vanilla extract powder, organic monk fruit, organic chia seed protein, sea salt and vanilla bean. FlavCity Chocolate lists twelve, adding banana powder, yeast protein, milk protein and two mushroom powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both avoid the things clean-label buyers usually screen for. Neither lists sucralose, acesulfame potassium, sugar alcohols, gums or natural flavors on the panels we read, and both describe their whey as grass-fed. FlavCity claims 0 g of added sugar and no GMOs; the non-GMO statement is a brand claim with no certifier named. Just Ingredients shows a Certified Gluten-Free logo on its bag, which we did not verify in the certifier's database.
          </p>

          <p>
            The functional extras are FlavCity's distinguishing feature, and they deserve some skepticism. The label does not say how much cordyceps or reishi is in a scoop, and the human evidence for either at small doses is limited, so we would not pay a premium for them. Just Ingredients' extra is chia seed protein, which adds a little plant protein but, at under 1 g of fiber per scoop, very little fiber.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fat and allergens are where Just Ingredients is less clean for some readers. Its 7 g of fat per scoop comes mostly from coconut milk, and it declares coconut as an allergen. FlavCity also uses coconut milk powder but declares only milk, and notes shared equipment with peanuts and tree nuts. Our Label Score, 43 for Just Ingredients and 23 for FlavCity, weights protein density and verified testing heavily, which is why the gap is wide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Tastes Better and Mixes Easier?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both brands sell mainly on taste, and buyer reviews for each are broadly positive; the difference is in the kind of drink. We have not tasted either, so this section draws on retailer reviews, the brands' own serving suggestions and what the labels imply about texture.
          </p>

          <p>
            FlavCity is built to drink like a smoothie. Banana powder, coconut milk powder and cocoa give it body and some natural sweetness, and the brand suggests shaking a scoop with milk or water. Its flavor list reads like a café menu: Chocolate, Vanilla Cream, PB&amp;J Strawberry, PB&amp;J Grape, Pumpkin Spice Latte, Vanilla Latte, Banana Bread, Butter Coffee, Cinnamon Roll and more, with several marked sold out at times.
          </p>

          <p>
            Just Ingredients leans on richness rather than fruit. Seven grams of fat from coconut milk makes a creamy, milkshake-style drink even in water, and buyers often describe it as dessert-like. Its eleven flavors include French Vanilla, Vanilla Bean, Chocolate, Snickerdoodle, Strawberries and Cream, Cookies and Cream, Lemon Birthday Cake and Maple.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweetener may decide it for you. FlavCity uses two forms of stevia, which some people taste as bitter or licorice-like. Just Ingredients French Vanilla uses monk fruit alone, though other flavors and retailer listings have shown stevia plus monk fruit, so check the panel. Neither lists gums, so both may settle if left standing; a shaker bottle or a quick blend handles that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="meal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Is FlavCity or Just Ingredients Better as a Meal Replacement?
          </h2>

          <p>
            FlavCity is closer to a breakfast in a bag, while Just Ingredients is closer to a protein powder with added richness. Neither is a formulated meal replacement with added vitamins and minerals, so neither should replace meals day after day without other food.
          </p>

          <p>
            FlavCity's 200 calories, 13 g of carbohydrate, 3 g of fiber and 4 g of fat per scoop, before any milk, look like a light breakfast. Shaken with a cup of milk it lands around 300 calories or more, which is a reasonable morning meal for many adults. The fruit and fiber are the point; you pay for them in protein density.
          </p>

          <p>
            Just Ingredients' 160 calories come mostly from protein and fat, with only 5 g of carbohydrate. That suits low-carb and keto eaters, and the brand does label it keto friendly. As a meal it needs something added, such as fruit, oats or nut butter, which is easy in a blender but adds work.
          </p>

          <p>
            For weight loss, neither is especially lean: about 182 to 200 calories per 25 g of protein, against roughly 125 for a flavored whey isolate. If calories per gram of protein matter most, look at the isolates in our <a href='/best-clean-protein-powder/'>best clean protein powder</a> guide. If the shake replaces a meal you would otherwise eat, the extra calories here are not a problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pros-cons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. What Each Brand Does Well and Where It Falls Short
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">FlavCity Protein Smoothie</h3>

          <p>
            FlavCity does convenience and transparency on collagen well. It states the 10 g collagen figure outright, sweetens with stevia only, claims 0 g of added sugar, and puts real banana and coconut milk in the bag. It is widely sold through its own store, Amazon, Target and TikTok Shop, per the brand's authorized-retailer page. The brand grew out of the food and cooking media following built around Bobby Parrish's YouTube channel, which its site still links, and the label reads like that: food first.
          </p>

          <p>
            It falls short on density, price and proof. At 51 percent protein by weight and about $3.00 per 25 g, it delivers less protein per dollar than almost any dedicated powder we track, and roughly 10 g of its 25 g is collagen. It has no third-party certification and carries a Prop 65 notice. The Amazon listing titles and the brand's panel disagree on protein, which is worth checking before you buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Just Ingredients Protein Powder</h3>

          <p>
            Just Ingredients does value and verification better. It is about 18 percent cheaper per 25 g, 62 percent protein by weight, and five of its flavors are in the NSF Certified for Sport registry. Its nine-ingredient list has no gums or flavor systems, and the French Vanilla uses monk fruit. The brand was founded by Karalynne Call, whom the company describes as a certified nutritionist and podcast host, per our review.
          </p>

          <p>
            It falls short on fat, collagen disclosure and flavor-level consistency. Seven grams of fat per scoop is a lot for a protein powder, the collagen share is not published, and the NSF listing does not cover every flavor, including French Vanilla. Panels also differ between the brand site and some retail listings. Our <a href='/just-ingredients-protein-powder-review/'>full Just Ingredients review</a> covers flavors and retail availability, and our <a href='/flavcity-protein-powder-review/'>FlavCity review</a> covers the plant-based version and packets.
          </p>
        </div>

