        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming both collagen blends hide the same amount</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                FlavCity states about 10 g of collagen per scoop; Just Ingredients lists collagen but no figure. Treating them as equal misreads both labels. With FlavCity you can subtract and see roughly 15 g of complete protein. With Just Ingredients you can only infer from ingredient order that whey is the larger share.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the NSF logo on any Just Ingredients bag</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The French Vanilla bag shows an NSF logo, yet the NSF Certified for Sport registry listed only Chocolate, Creamy Peanut Butter Chocolate, Snickerdoodle, Strawberries and Cream and Vanilla Bean when we checked. If certification matters because you are tested, search the registry for the exact flavor rather than relying on bag artwork.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging value by bag price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                FlavCity's $59.99 bag looks cheaper than Just Ingredients' $64.99 bag, but it holds 20 servings against 30 and 500 g of protein against 660 g. Per 25 g, FlavCity costs about $3.00 and Just Ingredients about $2.46. Always divide by servings and protein grams before comparing sticker prices.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on FlavCity's Amazon title for the protein figure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                FlavCity's Amazon store titled its bags 20 g of protein plus 10 g of collagen, while the brand's own panel shows 25 g total. Those cannot both describe the same scoop. Read the nutrition panel on the bag you receive, and use it, not a listing title, for your tracking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating either one as a lean diet shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both land around 182 to 200 calories per 25 g of protein, well above a flavored whey isolate. FlavCity's calories come from banana, cocoa and coconut; Just Ingredients' come largely from 7 g of coconut fat. That is fine for a meal, but it adds up fast if the shake is an extra on top of meals.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>FlavCity vs Just Ingredients: which is better?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most buyers, Just Ingredients. It costs about $2.46 per 25 g of protein against $3.00 for FlavCity at brand-site prices, it is about 62 percent protein by weight against 51 percent, and five of its flavors are in the NSF Certified for Sport registry. FlavCity is better if you want a breakfast-style smoothie with fruit, fiber and a stated 10 g of collagen.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Just Ingredients protein NSF certified?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Some flavors are. The NSF Certified for Sport registry listed Chocolate, Creamy Peanut Butter Chocolate, Snickerdoodle, Strawberries and Cream and Vanilla Bean when our database was checked on 1 November 2026. French Vanilla was not listed, despite an NSF logo on its bag image. Search the registry for your exact flavor before relying on it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is FlavCity protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not that we could find. FlavCity's product pages make no testing claim, and we found no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project listing. The bag carries a California Prop 65 notice for lead and cadmium, which is a legal warning rather than a test result. Drug-tested athletes should choose a certified product instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do FlavCity and Just Ingredients both have collagen?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. FlavCity says about 10 g of each 25 g scoop is grass-fed collagen. Just Ingredients lists grass-fed collagen third in its French Vanilla ingredients but does not publish the amount. Collagen counts toward the protein total but lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, so it does less for muscle than whey.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which has fewer calories, FlavCity or Just Ingredients?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Just Ingredients, per scoop and per gram of protein. A scoop is 160 calories against FlavCity's 200, per the labels at the time of writing. Scaled to 25 g of protein, Just Ingredients is about 182 calories and FlavCity 200. Just Ingredients has more fat, 7 g against 4 g, while FlavCity has far more carbohydrate, 13 g against 5 g.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweeteners do FlavCity and Just Ingredients use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  FlavCity Chocolate uses organic stevia leaf extract (Reb A) and stevia Reb M. Just Ingredients French Vanilla lists organic monk fruit extract only, though other flavors and some retailer listings have shown stevia plus monk fruit. Neither lists sucralose, acesulfame potassium or sugar alcohols on the panels we read.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Where can you buy FlavCity and Just Ingredients?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  FlavCity lists its own store, Amazon, Target and TikTok Shop as authorized retailers; Costco is not on that list. Just Ingredients sells through its own site, Amazon, Whole Foods Market, Sprouts and Target, per our review. On Amazon, check the seller and compare the listed panel with the brand's, since protein figures have differed.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

